          "title": "The Big Picture (5 Lines)",
          "markdown": "## 1. The Big Picture (5 Lines)\n1. A matrix is simply a rectangular table of numbers arranged in rows and columns that helps us store and manipulate linear equations compactly.\n2. Three simple row moves (swap, multiply by non-zero number, add multiple of another row) simplify any matrix without changing its essential information (**rank**).\n3. Reducing an augmented matrix $[A|B]$ to Echelon form tells us immediately whether equations have **zero, one, or infinite solutions** (Rouché–Capelli theorem).\n4. **Eigenvalues** and **eigenvectors** reveal special directions where a matrix stretches space without rotating it, powering Google PageRank, vibrations, and **diagonalization**.\n5. **Cayley–Hamilton** states every square matrix obeys its own characteristic polynomial, giving a fast formula for **$A^{-1}$** and huge matrix powers like **$A^8$**, while orthogonal diagonalization reduces quadratic forms to clean **canonical sums of squares** (`extra`)."
