          "id": "ch-2-definitions-theorems-formulas-with-one-line-meanings",
          "number": "2",
          "title": "Definitions, Theorems & Formulas (With One-Line Meanings)",
          "markdown": "## 2. Definitions, Theorems & Formulas (With One-Line Meanings)\n\n### A. Matrix Fundamentals & Special Types\n- **Order ($m \\times n$):** Number of rows $\\times$ number of columns. *Meaning: A $2 \\times 3$ matrix has 2 horizontal rows and 3 vertical columns.*\n- **Matrix vs Determinant:** A matrix is an array with no single value; $|A|$ is a scalar number defined only for square matrices.\n- **Trace $\\operatorname{tr}(A)$:** $\\sum_{i=1}^n a_{ii} = a_{11} + a_{22} + \\dots + a_{nn}$. *Meaning: Sum of the main diagonal entries (equals the sum of eigenvalues).*\n- **Symmetric ($A' = A$) & Skew-Symmetric ($A' = -A$):** Mirror across main diagonal stays equal (symmetric) or flips sign with $0$ diagonal (skew-symmetric).\n- **Orthogonal Matrix ($AA' = A'A = I \\iff A' = A^{-1}$):** Transpose acts directly as the inverse; preserves lengths and angles.\n- **Nilpotent ($A^k = O$) & Involutory ($A^2 = I$):** Nilpotent vanishes on repeated self-multiplication (usually $A^2=O$ in exam); involutory returns to identity when squared.\n- **Singular Matrix ($|A| = 0$) & Non-Singular ($|A| \\neq 0$):** Singular means the matrix collapses space and has **no inverse**; non-singular means $A^{-1}$ exists.\n- **Conjugate Transpose ($A^\\theta = (\\bar{A})' = \\overline{(A')}$):** Take complex conjugate of every entry and transpose. *Meaning: Complex generalization of transpose.*\n- **Hermitian ($A^\\theta = A$), Skew-Hermitian ($A^\\theta = -A$), Unitary ($A^\\theta A = I$):** Complex analogues of symmetric, skew-symmetric, and orthogonal matrices. *(Note: Diagonal of Hermitian is always real; diagonal of Skew-Hermitian is $0$ or purely imaginary.)*\n\n### B. Elementary Transformations, Inverse, Rank & Normal Form\n- **3 Elementary Row (or Column) Moves:** (1) $R_i \\leftrightarrow R_j$ (swap), (2) $R_i \\to k R_i$ ($k \\neq 0$), (3) $R_i \\to R_i + k R_j$. *Meaning: Legal moves that preserve row space and matrix rank.*\n- **Rank $\\rho(A)$:** Number of non-zero rows in Row Echelon Form (or largest order of non-zero minor). *Meaning: True number of independent equations in $A$.*\n  - Key Bounds: $\\rho(O) = 0$; for $m \\times n$ matrix, $\\rho(A) \\le \\min(m, n)$; if $n \\times n$ and $|A| \\neq 0$, then $\\rho(A) = n$.\n- **Normal Form ($[I_r, O]$ blocks):** Simplest canonical block $\\begin{bmatrix} I_r & O \\\\ O & O \\end{bmatrix}$ obtained using **both row and column moves**, giving $\\rho(A) = r$.\n- **PAQ Theorem:** If row moves convert left $I_m \\to P$ and column moves convert right $I_n \\to Q$, then $PAQ = \\text{Normal Form}$. *(When $PAQ = I$, $A^{-1} = QP$.)*\n- ** Rank–Nullity Theorem:** For an $m \\times n$ matrix, $\\text{rank}(A) + \\text{nullity}(A) = n$ (number of columns). *Meaning: Rank plus number of free parameters equals total variables.*\n\n### C. Linear Systems & Linear Dependence\n- **Rouché–Capelli Theorem ($AX = B$, $n$ unknowns):**\n  - $\\rho(A) \\neq \\rho([A|B])$ $\\implies$ **No solution (Inconsistent)**.\n  - $\\rho(A) = \\rho([A|B]) = n$ $\\implies$ **Unique solution**.\n  - $\\rho(A) = \\rho([A|B]) = r < n$ $\\implies$ **Infinite solutions** with $(n - r)$ arbitrary parameters.\n- **Homogeneous System ($AX = 0$):** Always consistent ($\\rho(A) = \\rho([A|0])$).\n  - $\\rho(A) = n$ (or $|A| \\neq 0$) $\\implies$ **Trivial solution only** ($x_1 = x_2 = \\dots = 0$).\n  - $\\rho(A) < n$ (or $|A| = 0$ for square $A$) $\\implies$ **Infinite non-trivial solutions**.\n- **Linear Dependence (LD) vs Independence (LI):** Vectors $X_1,\\dots,X_k$ are LD iff there exist scalars $\\lambda_1,\\dots,\\lambda_k$ (not all zero) with $\\sum \\lambda_i X_i = 0$. Using matrix $A = [X_1 \\dots X_k]$, **$\\rho(A) = k \\implies$ LI**, and **$\\rho(A) < k \\implies$ LD**.\n\n### D. Eigenvalues, Eigenvectors, Cayley–Hamilton, Diagonalization & Quadratic Forms\n- **Characteristic Equation ($|A - \\lambda I| = 0$):** For a $3 \\times 3$ matrix:\n  $$\\lambda^3 - S_1 \\lambda^2 + S_2 \\lambda - |A| = 0$$\n  where $S_1 = \\operatorname{tr}(A)$ and $S_2 = A_{11} + A_{22} + A_{33}$ (sum of principal diagonal minors).\n- **Eigenvalue Properties:** $\\sum \\lambda_i = \\operatorname{tr}(A)$; $\\prod \\lambda_i = |A|$; eigenvalues of $A^T$ match $A$; polynomial $f(A)$ has eigenvalues $f(\\lambda_i)$; $A^{-1}$ has eigenvalues $1/\\lambda_i$; eigenvalues of diagonal/triangular matrix are its diagonal entries.\n- **Cayley–Hamilton Theorem:** Every square matrix satisfies its own characteristic equation: $A^3 - S_1 A^2 + S_2 A - |A| I = O$.\n- ** Similarity & Diagonalization ($P^{-1} A P = D$):** If $n \\times n$ matrix $A$ has $n$ linearly independent eigenvectors, placing them as columns of $P$ gives diagonal $D = \\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1,\\dots,\\lambda_n)$. Then $A^m = P D^m P^{-1}$.\n- ** Quadratic Form to Canonical Form:** $Q = X^T A X$ ($A$ symmetric, $a_{ij} = \\frac{1}{2}\\text{coeff of } x_i x_j$) reduces via **orthogonal** matrix $P$ (normalized orthogonal eigenvectors, $P^{-1} = P^T$) under $X = PY$ to **Canonical Form**:\n  $$Q = \\lambda_1 y_1^2 + \\lambda_2 y_2^2 + \\lambda_3 y_3^2$$\n  (*Definiteness: Positive definite if all $\\lambda_i > 0$; Positive semi-definite if $\\lambda_i \\ge 0$ with some $0$; Indefinite if mixed signs.*)"
