          "id": "ch-3-step-by-step-methods",
          "number": "3",
          "title": "Step-by-Step Methods",
          "markdown": "## 3. Step-by-Step Methods\n\n### Method 1: Inverse by Elementary Row Transformations ($A^{-1}$)\n1. Write $A = I A$ (or augmented $[A \\mid I]$).\n2. Use **ONLY row operations** (never mix column operations!).\n3. Work left-to-right column by column: get a `1` at pivot $(i,i)$ (swap rows or scale), make all entries below and above it `0`.\n4. Stop when the left matrix becomes $I$; the matrix multiplying $A$ on the right is **$A^{-1}$**.\n\n### Method 2: Rank by Echelon Form vs Normal Form ($PAQ$)\n- **Echelon (Row-Only):** Use row moves to turn all entries *below* the leading pivot of each row into `0`. Count non-zero rows = **$\\rho(A)$**.\n- **Normal Form ($PAQ$):** Write $A = I_m A I_n$. Every **row move** changes $A$ and the **left $I_m$**; every **column move** changes $A$ and the **right $I_n$**. Reduce $A$ to $\\begin{bmatrix} I_r & O \\\\ O & O \\end{bmatrix}$; then left matrix is $P$, right matrix is $Q$, and rank is $r$.\n\n### Method 3: Finding Eigenvalues & Eigenvectors\n1. Compute $S_1 = \\text{sum of diagonal}$, $S_2 = \\text{sum of } 2\\times 2 \\text{ diagonal minors}$, and $|A|$.\n2. Solve $\\lambda^3 - S_1 \\lambda^2 + S_2 \\lambda - |A| = 0$ for $\\lambda_1, \\lambda_2, \\lambda_3$.\n3. For each eigenvalue $\\lambda$, solve $(A - \\lambda I) X = 0$ by row reduction (or 2-row cross-multiplication) to obtain a non-zero eigenvector $X$.\n\n### Method 4: Cayley–Hamilton Inverse & Higher Matrix Polynomials\n1. Form characteristic equation and substitute $A \\to \\lambda$, keeping constant term with $I$: $A^3 - S_1 A^2 + S_2 A - |A|I = O$.\n2. **For $A^{-1}$:** Pre-multiply by $A^{-1}$ (requires $|A|\\neq 0$): $|A| A^{-1} = A^2 - S_1 A + S_2 I$.\n3. **For large polynomial $P(A)$:** Divide $P(A)$ by characteristic polynomial $C(A)$ so $P(A) = Q(A)\\cdot C(A) + R(A) = R(A)$ (at most degree $n-1$).\n\n### Method 5: Orthogonal Reduction of Quadratic Form to Canonical Form\n1. Construct symmetric matrix $A$ where diagonal = coefficients of $x_i^2$ and off-diagonal $a_{ij} = \\frac{1}{2}(\\text{coeff of } x_i x_j)$.\n2. Find eigenvalues $\\lambda_1, \\lambda_2, \\lambda_3$ and mutually orthogonal **unit eigenvectors** $e_1, e_2, e_3$ (apply Gram–Schmidt if repeated eigenvalue).\n3. Form orthogonal modal matrix $P = [e_1\\ e_2\\ e_3]$. Then $X = PY$ gives **Canonical Form** $Q = \\lambda_1 y_1^2 + \\lambda_2 y_2^2 + \\lambda_3 y_3^2$."
