          "id": "ch-6-common-mistakes-to-avoid-in-exam",
          "number": "6",
          "title": "Common Mistakes to Avoid in Exam",
          "markdown": "## 6. Common Mistakes to Avoid in Exam\n- Applying Euler's theorem directly to $u = \\sin^{-1}(\\dots)$ **before** rewriting $\\sin u = f(x,y)$.\n- Writing the wrong sign in **implicit Jacobians**: $(-1)^n$ where $n = 2$ gives $+1$, while $n = 3$ gives **$-1$**!\n- Stopping **Maxima/Minima** after finding just 1 or 2 stationary points *(always test ALL points)*.\n- In **Error Approximation** (*\"maximum error\"*), subtracting negative powers instead of **adding absolute values** ($|\\text{coeff}|$).\n- Expanding **Maclaurin** ($x=0$) for values far away from $0$ like $\\tan(46^\\circ 30')$ *(always center Taylor at nearby $a = \\pi/4$)*."
